/* First run */
"onboarding.window.title" = "First run";
"onboarding.step1.title" = "Lazy Switcher";
"onboarding.step1.body" = "Spots a word typed in the wrong keyboard layout and fixes it.

ghbdtn → привет     руддщ → hello

It switches the layout too, so the rest of the sentence comes out right.";
"onboarding.step1.primary" = "Next";
"onboarding.step2.title" = "Permission needed";
"onboarding.step2.body" = "macOS will not let a program see what you type without your consent.

What that means: the app sees keystrokes. What you type lives in memory for a few seconds and is wiped — it goes into no file and no log.

What it does not see: when the system turns on secure input — the login window, a password manager, an ordinary app's password field — the keystrokes never reach it at all.

On websites no browser turns that mode on. There a password field is recognised from the page markup: that works in Safari and does not in Chrome or Firefox, so those default to manual correction only.

Press the button, switch «Lazy Switcher» on in the list, and come back.";
"onboarding.step2.primary" = "Open Accessibility settings";
"onboarding.step2.secondary" = "Skip";
"onboarding.step3.title" = "All set";
"onboarding.step3.body" = "Type a word in the wrong layout and press space — it fixes itself.

Double Shift: with a selection, converts it; right after a correction, undoes it; otherwise fixes the last word.

Left and right Shift together pauses the app.

The gesture, the sound and the app list are all adjustable in Settings.";
